License: MIT
Comment:
 New files added by this fork (not present upstream): i18n runtime
 lookup tables, the "Design" appearance system, encrypted auto-login
 password storage, and password-protected session export/import.
 Everywhere else in the tree, this fork's changes are small patches
 embedded inside existing upstream files (see git history at the
 Source URL above for exactly what changed) and are covered by the
 catch-all "Files: *" stanza above rather than a separate copyright
 line, consistent with upstream PuTTY's own single-notice convention.

Files: unix/distro_icons.h
Copyright: 2024 Bjorn Lammers, Meier Lukas, Thomas Camlong, and Homarr Labs
 (31 of 38 embedded icons, sourced from dashboard-icons -- see Comment)
 Simple Icons contributors, CC0 (3 embedded icons: elementary, popos,
 slackware -- see Comment)
 selfhst/icons contributors (1 embedded icon: freebsd -- see Comment)
 Papirus Development Team (1 embedded icon: raspbian -- see Comment)
 2026 Darth Vader <max232san@gmail.com> (the "question", "astra" and
 "dropbear" icons, all self-drawn -- see Comment)
License: Apache-2.0 and CC0-1.0 and CC-BY-4.0 and GPL-3.0-or-later and MIT
Comment:
 RESOLVED as of 2026-08-01, see debian/README.Debian. All 38 icons in
 this header are now traced to a free/open source or are this fork's
 own original artwork, across four free/open icon catalogues plus
 three self-drawn icons:
 .
  * Apache-2.0, github.com/homarr-labs/dashboard-icons (31 icons):
    alma, alpine, amazon, arch, centos, cisco, debian, fedora, gentoo,
    kali, linux, manjaro, mikrotik, mint, nixos, openwrt, opnsense,
    oracle, pfsense, proxmox, qnap, redhat, rocky, suse, synology,
    truenas, ubuntu, vmware, void, windows, zorin -- confirmed by
    matching filenames against that project's metadata.json AND
    visually diffing the embedded pixel data against freshly
    downloaded originals (exact match for debian/ubuntu; synology
    matches the same family, "-dsm" variant).
  * CC0, simple-icons (simpleicons.org) (3 icons): elementary, popos,
    slackware -- confirmed by rendering their canonical SVGs and
    visually matching the glyph shape against the embedded pixel data.
  * CC-BY-4.0, github.com/selfhst/icons (1 icon): freebsd -- confirmed
    by visually matching the "Beastie" silhouette against a freshly
    downloaded original (same shape, this fork's copy is a darker
    recolour).
  * GPL-3.0-or-later, github.com/PapirusDevelopmentTeam/papirus-icon-theme
    (1 icon): raspbian -- confirmed by visually matching the raspberry
    silhouette (same drupelet/leaf shape, this fork's copy is a
    monochrome recolour). Papirus itself is already an official Debian
    package (papirus-icon-theme, maintained by the Debian Desktop Theme
    Team), which is good precedent that this exact asset is considered
    archive-acceptable.
 .
 Those 36 are effectively covered by their respective free licences,
 and the remaining 2 are this fork's own original artwork:
 .
  * "astra" -- replaced with original artwork after it turned out the
    official Astra Linux / "Группа Астра" press kit
    (astra.ru/about/press-center/materials/) has a direct logo
    download but states no usage licence at all -- a normal
    commercial trademark, unlike the community-project logos above
    that come with explicit reuse permission, so using it would have
    been a real unresolved trademark risk, not just an attribution
    gap. Also checked Wikimedia Commons directly: the only "Astra"
    hits there are an unrelated satellite company's PD-textlogo and
    an unrelated art photo, neither usable. Replacement is a plain
    blue "A" on a white rounded-square badge, matching the flat-badge
    style of "question" (see FEATURES.md Credits).
  * "dropbear" -- self-drawn from the start (a generic "DR" letter-
    badge, same flat-badge style), not sourced from any third-party
    catalogue in the first place, so there was never anything to
    trace here.
 .
 All 38 icons are now covered; splitting this single generated header
 back into per-icon source files with individual copyright records
 (so each icon could be declared under its own specific licence
 stanza instead of this one catch-all) is still outstanding but no
 longer blocks DFSG-freeness -- see FEATURES.md and
 debian/README.Debian.

